        "ever_jailed",
        "composite_wealth_1985",
        "composite_wealth_1996",
        "sex"
      ],
      "relations": "Wealth growth is composite_wealth_1996 minus composite_wealth_1985. Within-tier jailed-minus-never-jailed median growth gaps become monotonically more negative: $0, −$8,269, and −$16,600. Sex-adjusted OLS mean gaps likewise widen: −$21,487, −$46,005, and −$60,141. OLS models ever_jailed × wealth tier plus male, with Tier 1 as the tier reference and female never-jailed Tier 1 respondents as the baseline."
    },
    {
      "text": "In the low/indebted wealth tier, incarcerated and never-incarcerated Black respondents have no median wealth-growth gap, but incarcerated respondents have lower sex-adjusted mean wealth growth.",
      "context": "Black NLSY79 respondents with composite_wealth_1985 ≤$0 (n=1,316); incarceration exposure during 1985–1994 and wealth growth during 1985–1996. Wealth tier is an income proxy, not measured income.",
      "variables": [
        "ever_jailed",
        "composite_wealth_1985",
        "composite_wealth_1996",
        "sex"
      ],
      "relations": "The bootstrap median jailed-minus-never-jailed growth gap is $0 (CI [−$500, $0]), attributed to both groups sitting at the $0 debt floor. The sex-adjusted OLS mean growth gap is −$21,487; this is an association, not a causal effect."
    },
    {
      "text": "In the modest wealth tier, incarcerated Black respondents have lower median and sex-adjusted mean wealth growth than never-incarcerated peers.",
      "context": "Black NLSY79 respondents with $0 < composite_wealth_1985 ≤$10,000 (n=1,120); incarceration exposure during 1985–1994 and wealth growth during 1985–1996. Wealth tier is an income proxy.",
      "variables": [
        "ever_jailed",
        "composite_wealth_1985",
        "composite_wealth_1996",
        "sex"
      ],
      "relations": "The bootstrap median jailed-minus-never-jailed growth gap is −$8,269 (CI [−$10,851, −$6,274], excluding zero). The sex-adjusted OLS mean growth gap is −$46,005. Both penalties exceed those in the low/indebted tier; associations are not causal."
